  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 383,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 182,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57482,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 81,
        "headingId": 182,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unlike their soft tissue counterparts, angiolipomas of the breast generally present as solitary masses, which are not associated with pain {28049578}. Breast imaging studies may show a homogeneously hyperechoic mass on ultrasonography {22084191}. The majority are located in subcutis overlying breast, rather than in breast parenchyma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-09-03T14:33:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 384,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 183,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57483,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 81,
        "headingId": 183,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Although more commonly presenting in females, cases can also occur in males {20034977}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-09-03T14:33:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 385,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 184,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57484,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 81,
        "headingId": 184,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-09-03T14:33:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 386,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 185,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57485,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 81,
        "headingId": 185,
        "headingText": "<p>Although angiolipomas presenting specifically in the breast have yet to be interrogated with modern genomic technologies, a recent next-generation sequencing study including 35&nbsp;soft tissue angiolipomas found that 80% harboured detectable subclonal mutations in <em>PRKD2</em>, the gene encoding the protein kinase PKD2 {28139834}. PKD2&nbsp;is a serine/threonine kinase that acts as a major relay node of signal transduction pathways, with a role in transducing proliferative signals in endothelial cells and in activating vascular endothelial growth factor pathways. Within tumour tissue, PKD2 is expressed in both adipocytes and endothelial cells {28139834}. Activating mutations in the catalytic domain of <em>PRKD2</em> are detectable at an allelic frequency of 3&ndash;15% (highest in the adipocytic cell fraction), supporting the hypothesis that clonal adipocytic mutant cells secondarily induce the characteristic vessel formation in this tumour. No other chromosomal or consistent RNA-level changes were identified, and this mutation was not seen in other types of fatty tumours.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-09-27T12:21:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 393,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 206,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57486,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 81,
        "headingId": 206,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The typical appearance is that of a tan/yellow to brown circumscribed lesion, more commonly located in the subcutaneous fat as opposed to within the breast parenchyma, with a mean size of &lt;&nbsp;2&nbsp;cm {21087987}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-09-03T14:33:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 387,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 186,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57487,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 81,
        "headingId": 186,
        "headingText": "<p>In most cases, angiolipomas are predominantly composed of mature fat, but they display obviously increased small vasculature clearly evident even at low magnification. Capillary-sized vessels are often arranged in clusters that are more common towards the periphery of the lesion; fibrin microthrombi are a distinctive histological feature that can be identified in some core biopsies and in the great majority of excision specimens {21087987}. Cellular subtypes (with &gt;&nbsp;50% vascular tissue) occasionally raise concern for angiosarcoma (in particular because they are more likely to present in older patients and with suspicious imaging features), but angiolipomas have monomorphic nuclei generally devoid of substantial cytological atypia or proliferative activity, and they lack infiltrative borders {28676174; 28049578}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-09-19T15:16:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 388,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
